Z||000000||-=shame=-||06-27-2000||07:47 AM||||POINT 1: I would like to know the best fix for the stereo. Sometimes stations come in great then I'll hit a bump and they're gone then i'll hit another bump and the stereo will say it's getting the station (stereo text next to the station or time) but I'll have no sound.<BR>POINT 2: I live in Indianapolis, famous for having the fourth (or fifth) worst roads in the country. Save your breath, I'm not moving. But I would like to know how to handle "off-roading" (hahaha) in my little SUV. Already replaced front shocks, it's too easy not to.<BR>POINT 3: What are the factory speaker sizes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-27-2000||01:20 PM||||POINT 1:  Beats me.<P>POINT 2:  Indy is waaay down the list of the worst roads in America.  Smooth as glass compared to lots.  Try driving in LA or I10 in Louisiana.  (or is it I12?  Can't remember.  The one that runs along/on top of the bayous.)<P>POINT 3:  All depends what year Fiero.  All of them have 4x10s in the dash.  The '84s and '85s had 3 1/2" speakers in the headrests and the '86 and up had no headrest speakers, but 4x6s in the sail panels.<P>BTW--In case you didn't know, there are alot of forum members who live within an hour or two of the Circle.  I live about1 1/2 hours north and BOILERMAKER lives down the street from you (figuratively speaking).||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||06-27-2000||03:18 PM||||First thing I would check would be the electrical connections at the stereo.  My guess is there is at least one loose or corroded connection that moves or gets bumped when you hit a bump.  Then check the connections at the speakers.  Or if its an old stock radio, the raido could be bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||-=shame=-||06-27-2000||08:33 PM||||OK monkeyman ... Datelin had it about two months back. however, you're right about New Orleans, it was #1 worst roads in america, but i can't beleive (being California raised myself) that LA has near the roads we do. They don't have the famous asphalt, body eating salt that we need in the winter. Plus there the construction can go year round, not crammed into 7 or 8 months.<P>Thats all for "Off The Topic" or whatever. Nice to know that the real enthusiasts (spelling?) are Hoosiers.<BR>BTW it's an '86. Had to ask because Haynes manuals SUCK! Need to order a Chilton's, why don't they sell 'em here?||||reg||6
Z||000004||DJRice||06-27-2000||10:57 PM||||What do you need a Chilton's Manual for, youve got us!<P>Actually Ive heard that the Chiltons isnt much better, but with both books together you can find out what you need to know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||06-28-2000||08:12 AM||||I picked up a Chiltons at a swap meet for $5 and it is a good addition to the Haynes but not a substitute. Chiltons has absloutely no wiring diagrams although it does have some nice blow-up pics of the different assemblies of our little beasties.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||10:16 AM||||Get a P22.  Between us, the P22, Chiltons, and Haynes, you should be able to fix anything.||||reg||1
